INFO  [Claude Code] ### Problem Analysis
INFO  [Claude Code] The issue was that Diffblue Cover was trying to create a Spring test context for `WebSocketClientEndpoint` (at src/main/java/net/finmath/smartcontract/valuation/service/websocket/client/WebSocketClientEndpoint.java:78), but this class:
INFO  [Claude Code] - Is a Jakarta WebSocket `@ClientEndpoint` (not a Spring bean)
INFO  [Claude Code] - Extends `jakarta.websocket.Endpoint`
INFO  [Claude Code] - Has complex dependencies (URI, username, password, WebSocketContainer)
INFO  [Claude Code] - Manages its own WebSocket sessions
INFO  [Claude Code] - Is not designed to work with Spring ApplicationContext
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Solution Implemented
INFO  [Claude Code] I created a custom Diffblue base class at:
INFO  [Claude Code] ```
INFO  [Claude Code] src/test/java/net/finmath/smartcontract/valuation/service/websocket/client/WebSocketClientEndpointDiffblueBase.java
INFO  [Claude Code] ```
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] This empty base class follows the same pattern as other Diffblue base classes in the project (like `WebSocketConnectorDiffblueBase.java` and `BasicAuthWebSecurityConfigurationDiffblueBase.java`). It signals to Diffblue Cover that it should:
INFO  [Claude Code] - NOT attempt to create a Spring ApplicationContext for this class
INFO  [Claude Code] - Generate standard unit tests instead
INFO  [Claude Code] - Use mocked dependencies (URI, Session, etc.) rather than trying to wire up Spring beans
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Next Steps
INFO  [Claude Code] The Maven build command requires approval in your environment due to permissions. You should run:
INFO  [Claude Code] ```bash
INFO  [Claude Code] mvn install -DskipTests -Dgpg.skip=true
INFO  [Claude Code] ```
INFO  [Claude Code] Or simply:
INFO  [Claude Code] ```bash
INFO  [Claude Code] mvn compile test-compile
INFO  [Claude Code] ```
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] This will compile the new base class and make it available to Diffblue Cover, which should then be able to generate tests for `WebSocketClientEndpoint.onClose()` without encountering the Spring ApplicationContext initialization error.
